1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,820
A coronary angiogram answers a question:
is there a narrowing or blockage in an

2
00:00:05,825 --> 00:00:08,377
artery that supplies the heart muscle?

3
00:00:08,657 --> 00:00:13,529
In this example, the cardiologist reaches
those arteries through the patient’s right

4
00:00:13,534 --> 00:00:18,695
wrist. We’ll follow the instruments to the
heart, see how contrast creates the

5
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,914
images, and return to the wrist for
recovery.

6
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:26,233
The heart muscle gets its blood supply
from the coronary arteries.

7
00:00:26,514 --> 00:00:31,306
Plaque can build up in an artery wall and
make the channel narrower.

8
00:00:31,773 --> 00:00:36,438
An angiogram may be planned after a review
of symptoms and other tests.

9
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:42,403
In some suspected heart attacks, the team
may need an urgent angiogram and may go on

10
00:00:42,408 --> 00:00:44,066
to treat the artery.

11
00:00:44,439 --> 00:00:49,101
For a planned angiogram, your team
explains the test and reviews your

12
00:00:49,106 --> 00:00:53,702
medicines, kidney health and any previous
reaction to contrast.

13
00:00:54,284 --> 00:00:58,392
Follow their instructions about food,
drink and medicines.

14
00:00:58,766 --> 00:01:03,314
In the cath lab, staff monitor your heart
rhythm and blood pressure.

15
00:01:03,688 --> 00:01:05,416
You are usually awake.

16
00:01:05,429 --> 00:01:08,655
The team can give medicine to help you
relax if needed.

17
00:01:08,936 --> 00:01:12,335
The radial artery runs on the thumb side
of the wrist.

18
00:01:12,709 --> 00:01:17,850
Here the team checks the right wrist and
uses ultrasound to see the artery.

19
00:01:18,131 --> 00:01:22,297
The skin is cleaned and the area is
covered with a sterile drape.

20
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:27,568
Local anaesthetic numbs the spot where the
artery will be entered.

21
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:33,710
Once the area is numb, a small access
needle enters the radial artery.

22
00:01:34,292 --> 00:01:39,167
Watch the tip cross the near wall and come
to rest inside the artery.

23
00:01:39,749 --> 00:01:43,161
That hollow needle gives the first wire a
way in.

24
00:01:43,372 --> 00:01:49,163
A short access wire passes through the
hollow needle and into the radial artery.

25
00:01:49,537 --> 00:01:51,671
The wire points up the arm.

26
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:54,782
The needle slides back out over the wire.

27
00:01:55,155 --> 00:01:59,646
Watch how the wire stays in the artery and
preserves the entry path.

28
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:03,629
The short radial sheath goes in over the
access wire.

29
00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:09,712
A tapered insert, called a dilator, sits
inside the sheath as it goes in.

30
00:02:10,086 --> 00:02:14,275
The team removes the short access wire and
the dilator.

31
00:02:14,648 --> 00:02:17,107
The outer sheath stays at the wrist.

32
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:20,695
It gives the next instruments a way into
the artery.

33
00:02:21,069 --> 00:02:25,095
A longer diagnostic guidewire passes
through the sheath.

34
00:02:25,678 --> 00:02:31,268
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, lets the
cardiologist see where the wire is

35
00:02:31,273 --> 00:02:36,716
going. The diagnostic catheter advances
over the long guidewire.

36
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:40,164
The short sheath remains where the team
entered the artery.

37
00:02:40,178 --> 00:02:44,620
From the wrist, the wire and catheter
follow arteries up the arm, through the

38
00:02:44,625 --> 00:02:48,710
shoulder, and into the large artery that
leads to the aorta.

39
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:54,314
Follow the tip as it comes around the arch
and down the ascending aorta toward the

40
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:55,641
aortic root.

41
00:02:56,223 --> 00:03:00,691
The coronary arteries begin just above the
aortic valve.

42
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:05,412
At the aortic root, the long guidewire is
withdrawn from the catheter.

43
00:03:05,624 --> 00:03:10,878
Here the curved catheter tip is positioned
at the left coronary opening, above the

44
00:03:10,883 --> 00:03:12,297
aortic valve.

45
00:03:12,764 --> 00:03:17,731
The cardiologist injects X-ray contrast
through the diagnostic catheter.

46
00:03:17,745 --> 00:03:22,178
For a moment, it outlines the channel that
blood travels through.

47
00:03:22,551 --> 00:03:27,771
Watch the left main artery fill and then
see two of its main branches come into

48
00:03:27,776 --> 00:03:28,633
view.

49
00:03:29,215 --> 00:03:32,151
Now look at the same moment on the X-ray.

50
00:03:32,431 --> 00:03:36,528
The dark branching lines show where the
contrast is flowing.

51
00:03:36,994 --> 00:03:42,553
In this wrist example, the same diagnostic
catheter is shaped to reach both openings.

52
00:03:42,834 --> 00:03:47,322
It comes back into the aortic root, then
the tip is repositioned at the right

53
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:49,009
coronary opening.

54
00:03:49,591 --> 00:03:54,721
The cardiologist injects contrast
separately at the right coronary opening.

55
00:03:55,187 --> 00:03:59,841
The right coronary artery and its branches
briefly appear on X-ray.

56
00:04:00,052 --> 00:04:03,879
The imaging arm can move around the
patient to record the arteries from

57
00:04:03,884 --> 00:04:09,660
different angles. On this example image,
the contrast-filled channel is thinner at

58
00:04:09,665 --> 00:04:14,734
one spot. Another angle helps the
cardiologist check where the narrowing is

59
00:04:14,739 --> 00:04:16,618
and how severe it looks.

60
00:04:17,084 --> 00:04:21,119
Sometimes a pressure measurement or
imaging from inside the artery helps

61
00:04:21,124 --> 00:04:23,908
answer what the angiogram alone cannot.

62
00:04:24,491 --> 00:04:29,618
If no major blockage is visible and
symptoms persist, the team may look for

63
00:04:29,623 --> 00:04:30,956
other causes.

64
00:04:32,026 --> 00:04:36,700
The next step depends on the findings, the
symptoms, and whether the situation is

65
00:04:36,705 --> 00:04:42,412
urgent. It may be medicines, another test,
angioplasty, or a discussion about

66
00:04:42,417 --> 00:04:44,052
bypass surgery.

67
00:04:44,519 --> 00:04:49,787
Angioplasty uses a balloon and may place a
stent to treat a narrowing.

68
00:04:50,161 --> 00:04:54,095
In selected cases the team can do that in
the same session.

69
00:04:54,306 --> 00:04:57,241
Here, the test ends after imaging.

70
00:04:57,615 --> 00:05:01,871
After the images are complete, the
cardiologist withdraws the diagnostic

71
00:05:01,876 --> 00:05:03,801
catheter through the sheath.

72
00:05:04,082 --> 00:05:06,622
The sheath is still at the access site.

73
00:05:06,671 --> 00:05:11,928
Staff position a wrist compression band
over the puncture as the sheath comes out.

74
00:05:12,511 --> 00:05:15,849
The band puts pressure over the site to
control bleeding.

75
00:05:15,854 --> 00:05:19,591
Staff check the puncture site and
circulation in the hand.

76
00:05:19,871 --> 00:05:24,362
They gradually reduce the band pressure
and remove it when it is safe.

77
00:05:24,945 --> 00:05:28,460
Staff check your pulse, blood pressure and
wrist.

78
00:05:28,672 --> 00:05:31,165
They may check circulation in the hand.

79
00:05:31,539 --> 00:05:37,121
After an uncomplicated planned test, many
patients can go home the same day.

80
00:05:37,402 --> 00:05:39,896
Others need longer observation.

81
00:05:40,270 --> 00:05:45,211
Ask what the angiogram showed, how to care
for the wrist, when to lift or drive

82
00:05:45,216 --> 00:05:48,348
again, and whom to contact with questions.

83
00:05:49,070 --> 00:05:53,640
Follow the written instructions for the
dressing, activity, medicines and

84
00:05:53,645 --> 00:05:54,687
follow-up.

85
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:58,531
A small bruise or mild soreness can
happen.

86
00:05:58,904 --> 00:06:03,694
Contact the care team promptly for
increasing redness or pain, drainage or

87
00:06:03,699 --> 00:06:04,672
fever.

88
00:06:05,255 --> 00:06:09,731
Call local emergency services for heavy
bleeding or bleeding that continues

89
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:15,690
despite firm pressure, rapidly growing
swelling, a newly cold or numb hand,

90
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:19,092
new chest pressure or sudden shortness of
breath.

91
00:06:19,674 --> 00:06:22,296
The wrist gives the team a route to the
heart.

92
00:06:22,345 --> 00:06:27,831
The catheter delivers contrast, the X-rays
show the coronary arteries, and the images

93
00:06:27,836 --> 00:06:29,935
help guide the next decision.

94
00:06:30,309 --> 00:06:34,716
Before you go home, make sure you know
your result and what to do during

95
00:06:34,721 --> 00:06:35,926
recovery.
